# What `n` really invokes on ELF-aarch64. 0 when the number is unassigned there — the
# call then simply does not exist and the kernel returns -ENOSYS.
# ⭐ THIS ARM IS THE POINT OF THE TABLE: most of the differing syscalls have an x86 number
# that IS a real, different aarch64 syscall (setresuid 117 = ptrace, fchmod 91 = capset,
# futex 202 = accept), and those are the silent-wrong-call cases a heuristic cannot see.
# ⛔ v6.6.5 — SOURCED FROM THE KERNEL, AND THAT IS WHAT MADE IT LIVE. Until this release
# it was built from the aarch64 stdlib PEER's declarations and was therefore structurally
# unreachable: `_row_wanted` emits a `_SYSX_MEANT` row only for x86 numbers the peer does
# NOT declare, so the two sets could not intersect and this arm answered 0 for every `n`
# that ever reached it — measured, every row warning and none carrying this text, from
# v6.5.51 to v6.6.4. It is now built from tests/data/syscalls/aarch64.tbl (committed UAPI
# facts), restricted to the numbers `_SYSX_MEANT` actually carries so every row here is
